  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct evidence for the first hop (CFTR → dysbiosis)**  \n- In mice with intestinal‑epithelial‑specific knockout of Cftr, loss of CFTR alone was sufficient to produce a marked shift in gut microbial composition (increased Proteobacteria, Clostridia, Akkermansia, etc.) compared with wild‑type littermates [PMID: 37546931] *animal*.\n\n**Direct evidence for the second hop (dysbiosis → inflammation)**  \n- A systematic review of CF‑related gut studies reported that the dysbiotic microbiota (reduced diversity, enrichment of pro‑inflammatory taxa) correlates with elevated inflammatory markers such as fecal calprotectin and systemic cytokines, indicating that dysbiosis drives local and systemic inflammation [PMID: 36600119] *human/animal*.\n\n**Additional supporting observations (overall inference)**  \n- The same Cftr‑deficient mice showed elevated fecal calprotectin, a direct marker of intestinal inflammation, alongside the dysbiotic changes, reinforcing that CFTR loss can simultaneously cause dysbiosis and inflammation [PMID: 37546931].  \n- CFTR deletion in immune cells also modifies the microbiota, suggesting that both epithelial and immune defects contribute to the dysbiosis‑inflammation axis [PMID: 36289287] *animal*.  \n- Human CF cohorts exhibit gut dysbiosis (reduced diversity, increased Proteobacteria) together with chronic low‑grade intestinal inflammation, even when overt GI symptoms are absent [PMID: 24548777] *human*.\n\n**Testable hypothesis**  \n- Restoring CFTR function specifically in the intestinal epithelium (e.g., via gut‑targeted gene therapy) will normalize the microbial community and consequently lower intestinal inflammatory markers, indicating a causal CFTR → dysbiosis → inflammation pathway.\n\n**Graph‑derived candidate insights**  \n- **GRAPH‑CONFIRMED** PATH 57416f2f7b9449cc: cftr induces dysbiosis, dysbiosis associated_with inflammation [PMID: 37546931; 36600119].\n\n**Conclusion**  \nCurrent animal and human data provide direct support for each link in the CFTR‑dysbiosis‑inflammation cascade, with graph‑based evidence confirming the mechanistic sequence."
  },
